# Runtime dependency pins for qrcheck: this file is the compile input for requirements.lock.
# Regenerate with: pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements.lock requirements.in
# The lock is the only authority for transitive versions: never hand-edit it, and always
# install it with --require-hashes so the whole hashed closure is verified.
# pip-tools 7.5.3 must be run under pip==24.0, because later pip releases removed
# pip._internal.utils.compat.stdlib_pkgs, which pip-tools imports.
click==8.1.8  # CLI structure, option validation, CliRunner test harness
httpx==0.27.2  # HTTP target adapter: explicit timeouts, connection reuse, loopback client
numpy==1.26.4  # seeded PCG64 generator and array ops for damage synthesis and measurement
opencv-python-headless==4.10.0.84  # secondary reference decoder, OpenCV detector; headless, no GUI libs
Pillow==13.0.0  # image IO, grid sampling, resize/rotate/warp/blur/JPEG/noise, PNG encode
# The floor is 13.0.0 and not the 10.4.0 pinned before: CVE-2026-112399 is an APNG whose first
# frame uses OP_BACKGROUND disposal, so PngImageFile._open() allocates and fills the whole IHDR
# canvas before Image._decompression_bomb_check() runs -- present since 8.1.0 and fixed only in
# 13.0.0 -- and the PNG a target returns is the untrusted image that reaches Pillow's decoder
# (qrcheck/targets/base.py, qrcheck/evidence/mux.py). The advisories this pin set still accepts,
# and why, are recorded with the other dependency decisions in docs/dependencies.md.
segno==1.6.6  # golden encoder used strictly as the expectation oracle
zxing-cpp==2.2.0  # primary decoder: payload bytes, EC level, content type, corner position
# zxing-cpp publishes no cp311 wheel for Linux aarch64, where qrcheck doctor blocks with
# exit 2 naming the missing distribution. That is a runtime check, so no environment
# marker and no fallback decoder are declared here.
# The transitive closure (anyio, certifi, h11, httpcore, idna, sniffio, typing-extensions)
# is deliberately absent: pip-compile derives it, and --require-hashes needs all of it
# hashed, so the lock alone owns those versions.
# Two conditional dependencies belong in the locks and never on a supported platform: click's
# colorama (Windows only) and segno's importlib-metadata (below CPython 3.10 only). pip-compile
# 7.5.3, like pip, drops a requirement whose marker is false for the generating interpreter --
# CPython 3.11 on Linux/macOS -- so it cannot emit them, and a regeneration of either lock
# removes them. They are maintained by hand in requirements.lock and requirements-dev.lock at
# their alphabetical positions and must be re-added after every regeneration, which is why they
# are not pins below.
# Re-adding them is the one hand-maintained exception to the rule above: copy the two stanzas
# from the committed locks, which remain the only authority for their exact versions. Each lock
# records colorama at the release the index resolves and importlib-metadata at the newest release
# whose requires-python still admits the low end of the marker's range (>=3.8 today; 8.6.0+
# require >=3.9 and 8.8.0+ require >=3.10). Neither is ever installed here, and
# importlib-metadata's own dependency zipp is never installed either, so no zipp stanza is
# recorded.
